digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56787-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether the Hyaloperonospora arabidopsidis RXLR effector HaRxL10 manipulates Arabidopsis by binding to and stabilizing the central clock transcription factor CHE, disrupting CHE transcriptional activity and circadian gene expression, and thereby weakening salicylic-acid-mediated plant immunity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Mechanistic links among pathogen effectors, CHE interaction, CHE stability, transcription-factor binding, circadian regulation, and immune outputs motivate testing whether an effector rewires clock-associated defense in Arabidopsis."];
  "Node_Case1" [label="(2,1,0) deduction-case: Currently the miniature size of most pathogens causes their life cycle to be strongly affected by environmental factors whose levels oscillate on a daily basis."];
  "Node_Case2" [label="(4,1,0) induction-case: Currently plants couple their immune system with the circadian clock to anticipate and prepare for time-of-day infections."];
  "Node_Case4" [label="(12,3,0) induction-case: Currently Hpa secretes effectors to counteract plant defences."];
  "Node_Case7" [label="(22,2,0) induction-case: Currently molecular and genetic data showed that HaRxL10 interacted with CHE to interfere with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation."];
  "Node_Phenom5" [label="(17,1,0) abduction-phenomenon: The effector named HaRxL10 interacted with several clock components including CCA1 HIKING EXPEDITION (CHE) in yeast."];
  "Node_Rule1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If environmental factors that affect pathogens oscillate daily, then pathogen colonization success will peak at specific times of the day."];
  "Node_Rule10" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If an effector perturbs a key clock component's transcriptional regulation, then it will act as a virulent effector to repress immunity and impact physiological processes."];
  "Node_Rule3" [label="(1,1,0) deduction-rule: If plants employ a defense strategy, then pathogens will employ strategies to counteract it to gain the upper hand."];
  "Node_Rule6" [label="(21,1,0) deduction-rule: If a pathogen effector targets CHE, then it will repress salicylic acid (SA)-mediated plant immunity, because CHE plays a crucial role in it."];
  "Node_Rule8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If the mechanism of perturbation involves altering CHE's stability, then the effector will stabilise CHE but repress its binding to downstream regulatory genes."];
  "Node_Rule9" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If an effector stabilises a transcription factor but represses its binding to downstream regulatory genes, then it perturbs its transcriptional regulation function."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Given that many pathogen life cycles are strongly affected by environmental factors whose levels oscillate daily, and given the rule that daily oscillation in pathogen-relevant environmental factors can make colonization success peak at particular times, pathogen colonization success is expected to show time-of-day dependence."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) If perturbing a key clock component's transcriptional regulation can repress immunity and affect physiological processes, then HaRxL10-mediated perturbation of CHE's transcriptional regulation predicts effects on plant immunity and clock-linked physiology."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) The coupling of plant immunity with the circadian clock, together with expected time-of-day dependence in pathogen colonization success, supports the view that the plant circadian clock contributes to temporally organized defense against infection."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) If the circadian clock contributes to plant defense timing and pathogens commonly counteract host defense strategies, then it is plausible that pathogens may interfere with the plant circadian clock."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because Hyaloperonospora arabidopsidis secretes effectors to counteract plant defenses and pathogens may interfere with the plant circadian clock, Hpa effectors are plausible candidates to investigate for interference with the plant circadian clock."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The observation that the effector HaRxL10 interacts with the clock component CHE in yeast makes HaRxL10 a plausible Hpa effector candidate for targeting CHE in the context of circadian-clock interference."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Under the premise that a pathogen effector targeting CHE would repress salicylic-acid-mediated plant immunity because CHE is important for that immunity, HaRxL10 targeting CHE predicts a possible perturbation of SA-mediated plant immunity."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Molecular and genetic evidence that HaRxL10 interacts with CHE and interferes with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation supports the conclusion that HaRxL10 perturbation of CHE involves altered CHE stability."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) If HaRxL10 perturbation of CHE involves altered stability, then the effector is expected to stabilize CHE and repress CHE binding to downstream regulatory genes; therefore HaRxL10 is inferred to stabilize CHE while reducing CHE binding to downstream regulatory genes."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because HaRxL10 is inferred to stabilize the transcription factor CHE while reducing CHE binding to downstream regulatory genes, HaRxL10 is inferred to perturb CHE's transcriptional regulatory function."];
  "Node_Rule1" -> "R1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "Node_Case1" -> "R1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Node_Case2" -> "R2" [label="induction-case"];
  "R1" -> "R2" [label="induction-common"];
  "Node_Rule3" -> "R3" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R2" -> "R3" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Node_Case4" -> "R4" [label="induction-case"];
  "R3" -> "R4" [label="induction-common"];
  "Node_Phenom5" -> "R5" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "R4" -> "R5" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "Node_Rule6" -> "R6" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R5" -> "R6" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Node_Case7" -> "R7" [label="induction-case"];
  "R6" -> "R7" [label="induction-common"];
  "Node_Rule8" -> "R8" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R7" -> "R8" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Node_Rule9" -> "R9" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R8" -> "R9" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Node_Rule10" -> "R10" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R9" -> "R10" [label="deduction-case"];
  "R10" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-common"];
}
